     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Two-neuron chain:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " in both divisions, preganglionic neurons in the CNS connect to postganglionic neurons in ganglia outside the CNS, whose fibers run to the effector organs."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic outflow:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " preganglionic cell bodies in the intermediolateral horn from T1 to L2 or L3; ganglia beside the spine: the sympathetic chain plus prevertebral ganglia (S. cervical, celiac, S. and I. mesenteric, aorticorenal)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic targets:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " long fibers to smooth musc of vessels, viscera, lungs, scalp (piloerectors) and pupils, to the ♡, and to sweat, salivary and digestive glands."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic outflow:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " from the brain stem w CNs III, VII, IX and X (vagus), and from the sacral cord @ S2 and S3; the vagus carries about 75% of all parasympathetic fibers."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic ganglia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " lie w.in the effector organs (ciliary, sphenopalatine, otic, pelvic, vagal), w postganglionic fibers only 1 or 2 mm long, so responses are specific and localized."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overall roles:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " sympathetic = catabolic (fight-or-flight); parasympathetic = anabolic (conserves and restores)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic effects:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " raises HR and contractility, bronchodilates, increases hepatic glycogenolysis and glucose release, basal met rate and musc strength, makes the palms sweat, and turns down functions ⍉needed for immediate survival such as digestion."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic effects:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stimulates GI secretions and motility (incl bowel evacuation), slows HR, reduces BP, controls erection."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Transmitters:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " acetylcholine from all preganglionic fibers, all parasympathetic postganglionic fibers and the sympathetic postganglionic fibers to piloerectors and sweat glands; NE from most sympathetic postganglionic fibers."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Horner syndrome signs:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis and hyperemia on the affected side."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Horner syndrome Eti:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " disrupt of the cervical sympathetic pathway from the hypothalamus to the eye — central (ex brainstem ischemia, syringomyelia, brain or cord tumor) or periph (ex Pancoast tumor, cervical adenopathy, neck and skull injuries, aortic or carotid dissection, thoracic aortic aneurysm)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Autonomic dysreflexia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " USU after cord inj above T6 (unlikely below T10): a noxious stimulus below the inj, most often bladder distention (often a blocked cath), provokes life-threatening HTN."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Dysreflexia mech:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stim below the inj triggers reflex sympathetic vasoconstrict and a rise in BP; the parasympathetic correction cannot travel below the cord lesion, so the HTN continues."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Dysreflexia signs:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " sudden HA, N/V, visual problems, nasal congestion, anxiety; profuse sweating, flushing and piloerection above the inj, dry pale skin below it."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
